{"qid":"Q7380487","names":["Kingdom of Pontus"],"title":"Kingdom of Pontus","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Pontus was founded around 281 BC by Mithridates I, scion of an Iranian-Macedonian noble family, on the southern Black Sea coast of Anatolia, with its capital at Amasia. The dynasty reached its apex under Mithridates VI Eupator (r. c. 120-63 BC), who expanded Pontic control over the Black Sea littoral, incorporated Armenia Minor, and launched three major wars against Rome; his first invasion of the Roman province of Asia in 88 BC, accompanied by the massacre of some 80,000 Italian residents, made him Rome's most dangerous eastern opponent. Pompey the Great defeated Mithridates decisively in 66 BC; the king fled to Crimea and died in 63 BC, and Rome incorporated the core Pontic territory as the province of Bithynia and Pontus.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Kingdom of Pontus is reasonably well attested through Hellenistic and Roman literary sources (Appian, Strabo) and numismatic evidence; the core coastal territories are clearly delineated, but inland Anatolian frontiers, especially in the earlier period, are reconstructed rather than precisely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Pontus","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Pontus-ancient-district-Turkey"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Pontus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Pontus"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Pontus","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Mithridates I Ktistes","nativeName":"Μιθριδάτης Α΄ Κτίστης","roman":"Mithridatēs I Ktistēs","start":-281,"end":-266,"